  import Plug.Conn

  require Logger

  alias Commonplace.Cell.Proof
  alias CommonplaceNext.{EditorCell, Organization, WorkspaceCell}
  alias CommonplaceNext.Cell.Supervisor, as: CellSupervisor
  alias CommonplaceNext.EditorCell.Mirrors
  alias CommonplaceNext.Organization.{EditorPlacement, Membership, SessionCell}
  alias CommonplaceNext.Realm.{EditorIssuer, Placement, Route, Router}
  alias CommonplaceNext.Web.AccessAuthentication
  alias CommonplaceNext.Organization.EditorPlacement

  @doc """
  Establishes the session for the identity `Web.AccessAuthentication` verified o
n THIS request.

  ⭐ This is the second half of R9's consuming door: the plug verifies, and this
 function converts
  the verified identity into a login that resolves a Membership and gets a Sessi
on Cell. It writes
  the login through `put_login/2` below -- production's ONE place where a `:logi
n` is written into
  a session, and since `R11a` THE ONLY CALLER OF IT. `login/1`, `login/2` and `o
pen_session_cell/2`
  -- the credentialless establishing path the two `/login/` routes reached -- ar
e deleted.

  ⛔⛔ IT DOES NOT WRITE THE SESSION CELL ID INTO THE PLUG SESSION, AND THAT IS
THE POINT.
  `config/config.exs` hardcodes `secret_key_base`, so anything stored in the sig
ned cookie is
  forgeable by anyone holding the source -- and a stored id would be READ BACK b
y `admission/1` and
  believed. The authenticated path stores nothing it can derive: `admission/1` r
ecomputes the id
  from the verified assertion on every request. Storing it would also make the d
erive rule
  UNTESTABLE, because the stored value and the derived value would always agree.

  ⛔ Returns `conn` untouched for a request with no verified identity or no conf
igured login for
  it, and still establishes a login whose identity holds no Membership -- R6's a
dditive rule. R9
  removed nothing; `R11a` removed the other door, and this is now the only way i
n.
  """
  def authenticate(conn) do
    with {:ok, human} <- AccessAuthentication.verified_human(conn),
         {:ok, login} <- AccessAuthentication.login(human),
         {:ok, entry} <- resolve_entry(login),
         true <- placed?(entry) do
      # ⭐ D1's OTHER HALF, and it is an observation rather than a store: this i
dentity resolves a
      # Membership and its Session Cell is ADMITTED by the Editor Cell at login.
 Its id is thrown
      # away on purpose; `admission/1` derives the same value again, from the sa
me input.
      _session_cell = open_identity_session_cell(entry, human)
      put_login(conn, login)
    else
      _unauthenticated -> conn
    end
  end

  @doc """
  The admission a NON-BROWSER caller obtains for a raw `assertion`, or `:error`.

  ⭐⭐ THE SAME PATH A BROWSER REQUEST TAKES, COMPOSED RATHER THAN RESTATED. It
verifies the
  assertion through the one verifier, resolves the login and the Session Cell th
rough
  `derived_principal/2` -- which is the same function `admission/1` uses on a re
quest carrying a
  verified identity -- and mints or carries the proofs the same way. A second co
py of "verified
  identity -> Membership -> Session Cell -> proofs" would be a SECOND TRUST DECI
SION, and two
  trust decisions drift apart in the direction nobody is watching.

  ⛔ IT IS NOT A HOST-LOCAL MINT AND MUST NOT BECOME ONE. It requires a VERIFIED
 HUMAN: no
  assertion, no admission. `HostLocalSession` is the principal for callers that
have no human, it
  is counted at its declared sites, and this function is deliberately not an alt
ernative to it --
  which is why it takes an assertion and nothing else.

  ⭐ It works on either kind of BEAM: with the workspace local it resolves the M
embership here,
  and without it the resolution crosses and this realm mints for its own Editor
Cell.
  """
  def admission_for_assertion(assertion) when is_binary(assertion) and assertion
 != "" do
    with {:ok, human} <- AccessAuthentication.verify_assertion(assertion) do
      case derived_principal(human, assertion) do
        {:ok, session_cell_id, entry} ->
          case cell_open_proofs(session_cell_id, entry) do
            {:ok, proofs} -> {:ok, [admission: {session_cell_id, proofs}]}
            _unmintable -> :error
          end

        {:remote, session_cell_id, proofs} ->
          {:ok, [admission: {session_cell_id, proofs}]}

        _unadmitted ->
          :error
      end
    else
      _unverified -> :error
    end
  end

  def admission_for_assertion(_assertion), do: :error

  @doc """
  The MEMBERSHIP RESOLUTION the WORKSPACE HOST performs for an assertion it veri
fied itself.

  ⛔⛔ IT RESOLVES AND DOES NOT MINT, and that is this function's whole subject.
 It used to return
  a `cell.open` Biscuit; a Biscuit is valid only against the key that signed it,
 the realm's
  issuing key is generated PER BOOT and is BEAM-local, and the Editor Cell this
admission is for
  lives on the REMOTE. The host was minting a capability for a resource whose au
thority is not its
  own, and the remote's `EditorCell.admit_session/3` refused every one of them a
gainst its own
  root. Now the host answers the only question it is the authority for -- IS THE
RE A MEMBERSHIP,
  AND WHAT IS IT -- and the remote mints for its own Editor Cell with its own ke
y.

  ⭐ ONE RESOLUTION, REUSED. The Membership is resolved through the SAME accepta
nce order the
  browser-facing path uses, and returned in its OWN record encoding (`Membership
.encode/1`) rather
  than as fields chosen for this frame: one definition of what a Membership is,
and the remote
  parses it with the parser the persisted read path uses.

  ⭐ The `session_cell_id` is the host's OWN derivation, and it is a CROSS-CHECK
 rather than an
  instruction: the remote derives the same id from the same identity and the cro
ssed organization
  id, and refuses on disagreement. A host that named some other session cell wou
ld be believed by
  nobody.

  ⚠️  Called from `Realm.Gateway`, which is a layering inversion worth naming: th
e gateway reaches
  into a Web module. The alternative was duplicating the resolution, and a share
d trust decision in
  an awkward place beats two trust decisions in tidy ones.

  ⛔ `assertion` is `nil` here ON PURPOSE. The workspace host never forwards onw
ard: if it cannot
  resolve locally there is no further host to ask, and a fallback would be a loo
p.
  """
  def membership_resolution_for_verified_human(human) do
    with {:ok, login} <- AccessAuthentication.login(human),
         {:ok, entry} <- EditorPlacement.fetch(login),
         {:ok, hosts} <- workspace_hosts(),
         {:ok, organization} <- Organization.configured(),
         {:ok, membership} <-
           Membership.resolve_owner(organization, hosts: hosts, member_id: entry
.login),
         true <- membership.member_id == entry.login,
         {:ok, session_cell} <-
           SessionCell.open(membership, entry.cell_id, AccessAuthentication.sess
ion_key(human)) do
      {:ok,
       %{
         session_cell_id: session_cell.session_cell_id,
         membership: Membership.encode(membership)
       }}
    else
      _unresolved -> :error
    end
  end

  @doc """
  The `cell.open` admission this browser session presents to its Editor Cell, as
 `Mirrors.open/3`
  options.

  ⭐⭐ FOR A REQUEST CARRYING A VERIFIED IDENTITY THE ID IS DERIVED, NOT READ. S
ee `principal/1`
  below: nothing in the plug session is consulted on that path.

  Returns `[admission: {session_cell_id, proofs}]` for a session that holds a Se
ssion Cell, and
  `[]` for one that does not -- the R6 additive case, where the login holds no M
embership. ⛔ A
  session that HOLDS a Session Cell but cannot mint returns an admission with NO
 proof rather than
  no admission at all, so a broken issuer refuses instead of silently falling ba
ck to the
  unadmitted path: those two are the same observation from outside and only this
 keeps them apart.
  """
  def admission(conn) do
    case principal(conn) do
      {:ok, session_cell_id, entry} ->
        case cell_open_proofs(session_cell_id, entry) do
          {:ok, proofs} -> [admission: {session_cell_id, proofs}]
          _unmintable -> [admission: {session_cell_id, []}]
        end

      # ⭐ THE WORKSPACE HOST ALREADY MINTED THIS ONE. On a BEAM that does not h
ost the workspace
      # Cell there is no signer whose token that Cell would accept -- two BEAMs
hold two per-boot
      # keys and one trust root each -- so the id and the proof both come back f
rom the host that
      # verified the assertion, and nothing here re-mints.
      {:remote, session_cell_id, proofs} ->
        [admission: {session_cell_id, proofs}]

      {:unmintable, session_cell_id} ->
        [admission: {session_cell_id, []}]

      :error ->
        []
    end
  end

  # ⭐⭐ DERIVE, NEVER SUPPLY. For a request carrying a VERIFIED identity the ad
mitted principal is
  # RECOMPUTED from that identity, and the `session_cell_id` in the plug session
 is not read at
  # all. `config/config.exs` hardcodes `secret_key_base`, so a signed cookie is
forgeable by anyone
  # with the source: a derivation whose input came out of the cookie would deriv
e a forged
  # principal exactly as happily as a real one, and R9 would gain NOTHING. The i
nput here is the
  # per-request verified assertion and nothing else -- not even the `:login` in
the session, which
  # is equally forgeable; the login is re-resolved from the identity too.
  #
  # ⛔ A request with NO verified identity keeps reading the stored id, unchange
d. R9 removes
  # nothing: the development login path behaves exactly as it did.
  defp principal(conn) do
    case AccessAuthentication.verified_human(conn) do
      # ⚠️  The RAW assertion is threaded alongside the verified struct because
      # `AuthenticatedHuman` deliberately carries no assertion bytes -- and the
workspace host must
      # be handed the assertion itself, never this BEAM's reading of it.
      {:ok, human} -> derived_principal(human, raw_assertion(conn))
      :error -> stored_principal(conn)
    end
  end

  defp raw_assertion(conn) do
    case Plug.Conn.get_req_header(conn, AccessAuthentication.assertion_header())
 do
      [assertion] when is_binary(assertion) -> assertion
      _absent_or_repeated -> nil
    end
  end

  defp derived_principal(human, assertion) do
    with {:ok, login} <- AccessAuthentication.login(human),
         {:ok, entry} <- EditorPlacement.fetch(login),
         {:ok, session_cell} <-
           session_cell_for(entry, AccessAuthentication.session_key(human), asse
rtion) do
      {:ok, session_cell.session_cell_id, entry}
    else
      {:remote, session_cell_id, proofs} ->
        {:remote, session_cell_id, proofs}

      # ⛔ THE SECOND COLLAPSE POINT ON THE SAME LANE, and it hid the first: `re
mote_admission/4`
      # can now answer `{:unmintable, id}`, and this `else` used to fold it into
 `:error` --
      # `admission/1` then returned `[]`, so repairing ONLY the inner clause cha
nged NOTHING and
      # measured as "the state is unreachable". Two shapes of "no proof" reached
 one `:error`.
      {:unmintable, session_cell_id} ->
        {:unmintable, session_cell_id}

      _no_session_cell ->
        :error
    end
  rescue
    exception ->
      # ⭐ ABSENCE HAS MORE THAN ONE CAUSE: "this identity has no Membership" an
d "the derivation
      # crashed" are both an unadmitted request from outside. Neither may fail t
he request.
      Logger.warning(
        "session cell not derived for verified identity: " <>
          Exception.format(:error, exception, __STACKTRACE__)
      )

      :error
  catch
    kind, reason ->
      Logger.warning("session cell not derived for verified identity: #{inspect(
{kind, reason})}")
      :error
  end

  defp stored_principal(conn) do
    with session_cell_id when is_binary(session_cell_id) and session_cell_id !=
"" <-
           get_session(conn, :session_cell_id),
         {:ok, entry} <- entry(conn) do
      {:ok, session_cell_id, entry}
    else
      :error -> {:unmintable, get_session(conn, :session_cell_id)}
      _no_session_cell -> :error
    end
  end

  # ⭐ THE ONE ACCEPTANCE ORDER, used by both the establishing path and the deri
ving path: owner
  # Membership resolved -> Session Cell opened. ⛔ THE LOGIN'S OWN Membership is
 resolved -- the
  # records are keyed per (organization, member) and an Organization may hold se
veral. A login
  # holding none resolves `{:error, :absent}` and logs in with no Session Cell,
unchanged: R6's
  # additive rule. The `member_id ==` check below is kept as a control on that r
esolution. Two orders would be two answers to "what is this
  # session's principal", and only one of them could be the admitted one.
  # ⭐ THE ESTABLISHING PATH IS LOCAL-ONLY, AND DELIBERATELY SO. `open_identity_
session_cell/2`
  # goes on to ADMIT the Session Cell to a local editor Cell, which needs a real
 Membership-backed
  # struct and not a remote host's admission. (`open_session_cell/2` said the sa
me and was the
  # CREDENTIALLESS half; `R11a` deleted it with its routes.) On a BEAM with no w
orkspace this
  # behaves exactly as it did: no Session Cell, and the login still succeeds.
  defp session_cell_for(entry, session_key) do
    case workspace_hosts() do
      # ⚠️  `CellSupervisor.hosts/1` returns `{:error, :not_running}` when this B
EAM does not hold
      # the workspace Cell, and `{:ok, hosts}` when it does. The hosts value is
OPAQUE here --
      # an earlier revision matched it as a non-empty LIST, a shape it does not
have, and every
      # local resolution silently took the remote branch.
      {:ok, hosts} -> local_session_cell(entry, session_key, hosts)
      _no_local_workspace -> :error
    end
  end

  defp session_cell_for(entry, session_key, assertion) do
    case workspace_hosts() do
      {:ok, hosts} ->
        local_session_cell(entry, session_key, hosts)

      # ⛔⛔ NEVER LOCAL PROVISIONING. A BEAM that does not host the workspace C
ell cannot resolve a
      # Membership, and inventing one here would be a SELF-ISSUED Membership --
a forgery that
      # erases R10a's durability discriminator. The only other move is to ASK th
e host that can,
      # and to send it the raw assertion so it resolves the identity ITSELF.
      _no_local_workspace ->
        remote_session_admission(entry, session_key, assertion)
    end
  end

  defp local_session_cell(entry, session_key, hosts) do
    with {:ok, organization} <- Organization.configured(),
         {:ok, membership} <-
           Membership.resolve_owner(organization, hosts: hosts, member_id: entry
.login),
         true <- membership.member_id == entry.login,
         {:ok, session_cell} <- SessionCell.open(membership, entry.cell_id, sess
ion_key) do
      {:ok, session_cell}
    else
      _no_session_cell -> :error
    end
  end

  # The remote leg. ⛔ It forwards the ASSERTION and nothing else about identity
: no login, no
  # member id, no session cell id. The workspace host verifies the assertion its
elf and resolves
  # the Membership from the identity IT verified. What comes back is that host's
 RESOLUTION -- the
  # Membership record as data -- and never a capability.
  #
  # ⛔⛔ AND THIS BEAM MINTS ITS OWN. The Editor Cell being opened is THIS realm
's, its authority is
  # THIS realm's issuing key, and a token signed by the host's per-boot key is r
efused here exactly
  # as a foreign root should be -- `x_realm_cell_open_grant_test` pins that refu
sal and it stays
  # pinned. Nothing that crosses is a key, a seed or a root, so `B-ROOTS` holds
on both realms.
  #
  # ⛔ NO SELF-ISSUED MEMBERSHIP AND NO LOCAL RECORD. The struct is parsed from
the crossed
  # encoding and used in memory; nothing is written, nothing is provisioned, and
 this BEAM never
  # decides that a Membership exists -- it only ever learns that the host resolv
ed one.
  defp remote_session_admission(_entry, _session_key, nil), do: :error

  defp remote_session_admission(entry, session_key, assertion) when is_binary(as
sertion) do
    case Route.cell_open_grant(entry.cell_id, assertion) do
      {:ok, %{session_cell_id: host_session_cell_id, membership: encoded}} ->
        remote_admission(entry, session_key, host_session_cell_id, encoded)

      # ⛔ AN ABSENT RESOLUTION IS AN ABSENCE, NEVER A MEMBERSHIP. A host that r
esolved none sends a
      # refusal frame and this returns `:error`, which is the R6 additive case:
no Session Cell.
      _unavailable ->
        :error
    end
  end

  # ⛔⛔ TWO OUTCOMES, TWO ANSWERS -- AND THIS `with`/`else` HAD ONE. A REJECTED
 RESOLUTION and a
  # FAILED LOCAL MINT AFTER AN ACCEPTED ONE both fell into `_unadmitted -> :erro
r`, so `admission/1`
  # answered `[]` and the request refused as `no_session_cell`: on a remote BEAM
, "this host says
  # you are not a member" and "you ARE a member and my issuer is dead" were THE
SAME OBSERVABLE.
  # ⭐ MEASURED, NOT REASONED (S3-AUTH, at `b1b8391`): with the remote realm's `
DirectoryIssuer`
  # killed after an ACCEPTED host resolution, `Session.admission/1` in that BEAM
 returned `[]`.
  # The local path never had this shape -- `admission/1` has kept the mint's fai
lure separate at
  # `:163` since the seam round -- and the remote leg is now written the same wa
y, which is the
  # whole change: an accepted resolution that cannot be minted for is `{:unminta
ble, id}`, the
  # tuple `admission/1` already handles, and A REJECTED RESOLUTION IS STILL `:er
ror`.
  defp remote_admission(entry, session_key, host_session_cell_id, encoded) do
    case accepted_resolution(entry, session_key, host_session_cell_id, encoded)
do
      {:ok, session_cell} ->
        case cell_open_proofs(session_cell.session_cell_id, entry) do
          {:ok, proofs} -> {:remote, session_cell.session_cell_id, proofs}
          _unmintable -> {:unmintable, session_cell.session_cell_id}
        end

      _rejected ->
        :error
    end
  end

  @doc """
  Whether a workspace host's crossed resolution is acceptable for THIS login, an
d the Session Cell
  it names. `:error` for every rejection.

  ⭐⭐ THE DECISION IS SEPARATED FROM THE MINT ON PURPOSE, AND THIS IS WHY: with
 `Mirrors.open/3`
  still admitting an absent `:admission` (R6's additive rule), a rejected resolu
tion and an accepted
  one produce THE SAME OBSERVABLE from outside -- the mirror opens either way. T
hat is the same
  masking that hid the cross-realm defect itself for a whole round. Every check
below is therefore
  reachable as a value rather than only as an outcome, so an arm can drive them
one at a time.
  ⛔ IT MINTS NOTHING, opens no store and writes no record: `SessionCell.open/3`
 derives an id and
  touches nothing. Making it public adds no authority to anything already inside
 this BEAM.

  The three rejections, each for its own reason:

  - the record does not PARSE -- `Membership.decode/1` is the parser the persist
ed read path uses,
    so a fabricated frame has to fabricate a VALID record, not merely a plausibl
e one;
  - the record names a DIFFERENT MEMBER than the login this BEAM resolved from t
he assertion it
    verified itself. ⛔ Without this, a resolution for some other member would b
e accepted for this
    login's Editor Cell;
  - the Session Cell this BEAM DERIVES disagrees with the one the host derived.
⭐ RECONSTRUCTION,
    NOT ACCEPTANCE: equal ids mean both sides agree about the organization and t
he identity, and
    the crossed id is therefore never load-bearing -- a host naming some other s
ession cell is
    refused rather than believed.

  ⛔ NO SELF-ISSUED MEMBERSHIP. Nothing here decides that a Membership exists; i
t only ever learns
  that the host resolved one, and refuses if that answer does not match what it
asked about.
  """
  def accepted_resolution(entry, session_key, host_session_cell_id, encoded) do
    with {:ok, membership} <- Membership.decode(encoded),
         true <- membership.member_id == entry.login,
         {:ok, session_cell} <- SessionCell.open(membership, entry.cell_id, sess
ion_key),
         true <- session_cell.session_cell_id == host_session_cell_id do
      {:ok, session_cell}
    else
      _rejected -> :error
    end
  end

  # ⭐ The MINTING half is here, on the Session Cell's side of the edge; the VER
IFYING half is
  # `EditorCell.admit_session/3`, in the Cell that grants the capability. They a
re separate
  # modules on purpose: a door that mints its own admission proves nothing about
 the holder.
  defp admit_to_editor_cell(session_cell, entry) do
    with {:ok, proofs} <- cell_open_proofs(session_cell.session_cell_id, entry)
do
      EditorCell.admit_session(session_cell.session_cell_id, proofs, entry)
    end
  end

  defp cell_open_proofs(session_cell_id, entry) do
    with {:ok, issued} <-
           EditorIssuer.issue_cell_open(
             Router.request_id(),
             session_cell_id,
             entry.cell_id,
             entry.root_directory_id
           ),
         {:ok, proof} <- Proof.new(%{"format" => Proof.format(), "token" => issu
ed.token}) do
      {:ok, [proof]}
    end
  end

  # ⚠️  MEASURED: this does NOT return `{:error, :not_running}` when the realm is
 down. A terminated
  # realm takes `Realm.Router.Registry` with it and `CellSupervisor.hosts/1` rai
ses
  # `ArgumentError: unknown registry`. That is a NORMAL condition -- the remote-
editor suite runs a
  # second BEAM with no workspace cell -- so it is an absence, not a fault, and
must not log.
  defp workspace_hosts do
    CellSupervisor.hosts(WorkspaceCell.descriptor().cell_id)
  rescue
    _exception -> :error
  catch
    _kind, _reason -> :error
  end

  # ⛔ THE ONE PRINCIPAL SINK IN PRODUCTION -- the single place a `:login` is wr
itten into a
  # session -- shared by the development path and the authenticated one.
  # `bin/check-dev-path-inventory.sh` requires exactly one and requires it to li
ve in this module;
  # two sinks would be two ways to become a principal. ⚠️  That gate reads SOURCE
 TEXT, so writing
  # the call's literal spelling in a comment counts as another sink and blinds i
t: do not.
  defp put_login(conn, login) do
    conn
    |> put_session(:login, login)
    |> put_session(:mirrors, %{})
  end

  # ⭐ TWO WAYS TO BE PLACED, and the second is the whole of `EC-1`'s seam. A CO
NFIGURED login is
  # placed by appearing in `Placement.logins()`, exactly as before. A DERIVED Ed
itor Cell can
  # never appear there -- it is provisioned after boot -- so it is placed by bei
ng HOSTED.
  # ⛔ The configured half is unchanged and is checked first: a configured login
's placement
  # decision does not depend on anything this round added.
  # ⛔⛔ ONE LOCALITY SOURCE, AND `hosted?/1` IS NOT IT. This function reached `
Placement.hosted?/1`
  # RAW in the first cut of `EC-1`, which widened the REFUSAL branch it claimed
not to touch: on a
  # restricted placement, a CONFIGURED login whose cell is running in this BEAM
but is outside this
  # realm's `cells:` list was refused at base and would have been admitted. That
 is
  # co-residence-is-not-placement AT THE ADMISSION SITE -- the same finding as `
Placement`'s, at a
  # second site, and the suite was green because no arm covered it.
  # ⭐ `Placement.local?/1` carries the `cells() == :all` guard, so asking IT is
 the only way this
  # decision stays one rule rather than two copies that drift.
  defp placed?(entry) do
    Enum.any?(Placement.logins(), &(&1.cell_id == entry.cell_id)) or
      Placement.local?(entry.cell_id)
  end

  # ⭐ THE ADDITIVE SEAM ITSELF. `Logins.fetch/1` is asked FIRST and its answer
is taken
  # unchanged, so the two configured logins take a path this round did not touch
. Only its
  # `:error` -- previously the end of the road, a silent non-authentication -- n
ow asks whether
  # this login holds an owner Membership, and if so serves it from a derived Edi
tor Cell.
  # ⛔ A session with no login resolves to nothing WITHOUT touching the store. `
entry/1` runs on every
  # authenticated request, so the unauthenticated case must not reach `Membershi
p.resolve_owner/2`:
  # that would turn "no login" into a store read on every anonymous request.
  defp resolve_entry(nil), do: :error
  defp resolve_entry(""), do: :error

  # ⭐ ONE DERIVATION. Since the re-seed every roster login's Editor Cell id com
es from
  # `EditorPlacement`, configured or not -- there is no `Logins` cell to prefer
first any more.
  # Provisioning stays on this path because it is the first moment the login is
known.
  defp resolve_entry(login) do
    case EditorPlacement.fetch(login) do
      {:ok, entry} ->
        # ⛔ PROVISIONING IS BEST-EFFORT AND MUST NOT GATE THE LOGIN. The entry
is DERIVED from
        # configuration, so it is correct whether or not its Cell happens to be
running; whether the
        # Cell is up is a separate question that `placed?/1` and the admission p
ath already ask.
        # ⚠️  Measured: making the login depend on it broke the standing rule tha
t a login succeeds
        # WITH THE WORKSPACE CELL DOWN and simply gets no Session Cell.
        _best_effort = EditorPlacement.ensure_hosted(entry)
        {:ok, entry}

      :error ->
        :error
    end
  end

  # ⭐ Opens and ADMITS the Session Cell a verified identity derives. Its id is
deliberately not
  # returned into the session: see `authenticate/1`.
  defp open_identity_session_cell(entry, human) do
    with {:ok, session_cell} <- session_cell_for(entry, AccessAuthentication.ses
sion_key(human)),
         {:ok, _admitted} <- admit_to_editor_cell(session_cell, entry) do
      {:ok, session_cell}
    else
      _no_session_cell -> :error
    end
  rescue
    exception ->
      # ⭐ ABSENCE HAS MORE THAN ONE CAUSE: an identity with no Membership and a
 crash in this code
      # are the same observation from outside -- a login with no Session Cell --
 and only this log
      # tells them apart. Neither may refuse the login.
      Logger.warning(
        "session cell not opened for verified identity: " <>
          Exception.format(:error, exception, __STACKTRACE__)
      )

      :error
  catch
    kind, reason ->
      Logger.warning("session cell not opened for verified identity: #{inspect({
kind, reason})}")
      :error
  end

  def authenticated?(conn), do: match?({:ok, _entry}, entry(conn))

  @doc """
  The entry for the login this session holds, resolved the SAME way `authenticat
e/1` resolved it.

  ⛔⛔ IT USED TO BE `Logins.fetch/1` DIRECTLY, AND THAT MADE `EC-1`'s SEAM SING
LE-SHOT. This function
  backs `authenticated?/1`, `cell_id/1`, `admission/1`, `mirror_grants` and ever
y `PageController`
  site, so it runs on EVERY authenticated request -- and for a login with a Memb
ership but no
  `Logins` row it answered `:error`. **`authenticate/1` wrote the login and `ent
ry/1` could not read
defmodule CommonplaceNext.Application do
  @moduledoc false
  use Application

  alias CommonplaceNext.CloudflareAccess.KeySource

  @impl true
  def start(_type, _args) do
    Supervisor.start_link(
      children(),
      strategy: :one_for_one,
      name: CommonplaceNext.Supervisor
    )
  end

  # ⛔ KeySource is CONDITIONAL, and the condition is not decoration. Its `init/
1` fetches the
  # configured JWKS over the network; dev and test configure no `:jwks_uri`, so
starting it
  # unconditionally would put an outbound HTTPS attempt in front of EVERY `mix t
est` run (and
  # every dev boot) for a process nothing in those environments asks a question
of. It is added
  # only where an operator has actually pointed it at a team endpoint.
  defp children do
    [
      CommonplaceNext.Realm.Gateway.Credentials,
      CommonplaceNext.Realm.Gateway.Listener,
      CommonplaceNext.Realm,
      CommonplaceNext.Web.Endpoint
    ] ++ if(KeySource.configured?(), do: [KeySource], else: [])
  end
end
defmodule CommonplaceNext.Web.AccessAuthentication do
  @moduledoc """
  ⭐ THE CONSUMING DOOR FOR A VERIFIED CLOUDFLARE ACCESS ASSERTION: the `:browse
r` pipeline step
  that reads the assertion off the request, verifies it, and establishes the ses
sion.

  Until this module existed `CommonplaceNext.CloudflareAccess` had ZERO callers
outside its own
  directory: `AssertionVerifier.verify/3` produced an `AuthenticatedHuman` that
nothing converted
  into a login. This plug is that conversion, and it is the ONLY one.

  ⛔ THE VERIFIED IDENTITY IS PER-REQUEST STATE AND NEVER SESSION STATE. It is a
ssigned onto the
  `conn` (`conn.assigns.cloudflare_access_human`) and never written into the plu
g
  session. `config/config.exs` hardcodes `secret_key_base`, so ANY value read ba
ck out of the
  signed cookie is forgeable by anyone holding the source. A derivation whose in
put came from the
  cookie would derive a forged principal just as happily as a real one -- the tw
o are the same
  observation from outside. `Web.Session.admission/1` therefore recomputes the S
ession Cell id from
  THIS assign on THIS request. (Fixing `secret_key_base` is a separate round and
 is not in scope
  here; this module is written so that round changes nothing about what it trust
s.)

  ⛔ NO HTTP, NO DISCOVERY, NO FETCH. The verifying key arrives through the same
 seam
  `AssertionVerifier` already documents and `assertion_verifier_test.exs` alread
y uses: the caller
  supplies a `kid` resolver. Here that resolver, and the issuer/audience/lifetim
e the assertion is
  checked against, come from application configuration read at call time -- the
way
  `CommonplaceNext.Logins` and `:diagnostics` are read. ⛔ WITH NO CONFIGURATION
 THIS PLUG IS
  INERT: it returns `conn` untouched, so an unconfigured deployment behaves exac
tly as it did
  before this module existed.

      config :commonplace_next, CommonplaceNext.Web.AccessAuthentication,
        issuer: "https://example.cloudflareaccess.com",
        audience: "application-audience",
        max_lifetime_seconds: 3_600,
        kid_resolver: &MyApp.keys/1,
        logins: %{"assertion-subject" => "developer"}

  ⛔ THIS ROUND REMOVES NOTHING. The credentialless development login routes are
 untouched and
  still work; an unverifiable or absent assertion leaves the request exactly as
it arrived.
  """

  @behaviour Plug

  require Logger

  import Plug.Conn, only: [assign: 3, get_req_header: 2]

  alias CommonplaceNext.CloudflareAccess.{AssertionVerifier, AuthenticatedHuman}
  alias CommonplaceNext.Web.Session

  @assertion_header "cf-access-jwt-assertion"
  @assign :cloudflare_access_human
  @session_key_namespace "commonplace-next.cloudflare-access.session"

  @impl Plug
  def init(options), do: options

  @impl Plug
  def call(conn, _options) do
    case verified(conn) do
      {:ok, human} ->
        conn
        |> assign(@assign, human)
        |> Session.authenticate()

      :error ->
        conn
    end
  end

  @doc "The request header carrying the Cloudflare Access assertion."
  def assertion_header, do: @assertion_header

  @doc """
  The verified identity of THIS request, or `:error`.

  ⛔ Reads the per-request assign only. There is deliberately no fallback to the
 plug session:
  a fallback would restore exactly the forgeable input this module exists to sto
p trusting.
  """
  def verified_human(conn) do
    case Map.get(conn.assigns, @assign) do
      %AuthenticatedHuman{} = human -> {:ok, human}
      _absent -> :error
    end
  end

  @doc """
  The configured login for a verified identity, keyed by its assertion `subject`
.

  ⛔ Keyed by SUBJECT, not by email: `AssertionVerifier` allows `email` to be ab
sent and
  normalizes it for display, and a display value is not an identifier.
  """
  def login(%AuthenticatedHuman{} = human) do
    with logins when is_map(logins) <- Keyword.get(configuration(), :logins),
         login when is_binary(login) and login != "" <- Map.get(logins, human.su
bject) do
      {:ok, login}
    else
      _unmapped -> :error
    end
  end

  @doc """
  The Session Cell discriminator DERIVED from a verified identity.

  ⭐ Deterministic in the identity and nothing else: the same verified human der
ives the same
  discriminator on every request of a session, and two identities derive distinc
t ones. ⛔ It
  deliberately excludes `asserted_at`/`expires_at` -- a discriminator that moved
 when Cloudflare
  reissued the assertion would silently rename the principal mid-session -- and
`email`, which is
  a display value the provider may change.
  """
  def session_key(%AuthenticatedHuman{} = human) do
    :sha256
    |> :crypto.hash(
      Enum.join(
        [
          @session_key_namespace,
          to_string(human.provider),
          human.issuer,
          human.audience,
          human.subject
        ],
        "\n"
      )
    )
    |> Base.url_encode64(padding: false)
  end

  @doc """
  Verifies ONE raw assertion, with no `conn` anywhere in sight.

  ⭐ THE WORKSPACE HOST'S ENTRY POINT FOR A FORWARDED ASSERTION. It runs the SAM
E resolver, the SAME
  issuer/audience/lifetime options and the SAME `AssertionVerifier.verify/3` the
 plug runs, because
  a second verification path is a SECOND TRUST DECISION -- and two trust decisio
ns drift.

  ⛔ Returns `:error` for an unconfigured deployment exactly as the plug does. A
 workspace host that
  cannot verify must refuse, never admit: an unconfigured verifier that accepted
 anything would turn
  the whole cross-realm path into the forgery it exists to prevent.
  """
  def verify_assertion(assertion) when is_binary(assertion) and assertion != ""
do
    with {:ok, resolver} <- kid_resolver(),
         {:ok, options} <- verification_options(),
         {:ok, %AuthenticatedHuman{} = human} <-
           AssertionVerifier.verify(assertion, resolver, options) do
      {:ok, human}
    else
      _unverified -> :error
    end
  rescue
    exception ->
      # ⭐ ABSENCE HAS MORE THAN ONE CAUSE: "the assertion is invalid" and "our
verifier crashed"
      # are the same observation from outside. Both refuse; only this log tells
them apart.
      Logger.warning(
        "forwarded cloudflare access assertion not verified: " <>
          Exception.format(:error, exception, __STACKTRACE__)
      )

      :error
  catch
    kind, reason ->
      Logger.warning(
        "forwarded cloudflare access assertion not verified: #{inspect({kind, re
ason})}"
      )

      :error
  end

  def verify_assertion(_assertion), do: :error

  defp verified(conn) do
    with [assertion] <- get_req_header(conn, @assertion_header),
         {:ok, resolver} <- kid_resolver(),
         {:ok, options} <- verification_options(),
         {:ok, %AuthenticatedHuman{} = human} <-
           AssertionVerifier.verify(assertion, resolver, options) do
      {:ok, human}
    else
      _unverified -> :error
    end
  rescue
    exception ->
      # ⭐ ABSENCE HAS MORE THAN ONE CAUSE. "No assertion on this request" and "
our verification
      # code crashed" are the same observation from outside -- an unauthenticate
d request either
      # way -- and only this log tells them apart. Neither may fail the request:
 R9 adds an
      # authenticated path and removes nothing.
      Logger.warning(
        "cloudflare access assertion not verified: " <>
          Exception.format(:error, exception, __STACKTRACE__)
      )

      :error
  catch
    kind, reason ->
      Logger.warning("cloudflare access assertion not verified: #{inspect({kind,
 reason})}")
      :error
  end

  defp kid_resolver do
    case Keyword.get(configuration(), :kid_resolver) do
      resolver when is_function(resolver, 1) ->
        {:ok, resolver}

      {module, function} when is_atom(module) and is_atom(function) ->
        {:ok, fn kid -> apply(module, function, [kid]) end}

      _unconfigured ->
        :error
    end
  end

  defp verification_options do
    configuration = configuration()
    issuer = Keyword.get(configuration, :issuer)
    audience = Keyword.get(configuration, :audience)
    max_lifetime_seconds = Keyword.get(configuration, :max_lifetime_seconds)

    if is_binary(issuer) and issuer != "" and is_binary(audience) and audience !
= "" and
         is_integer(max_lifetime_seconds) and max_lifetime_seconds > 0 do
      {:ok,
       [
         issuer: issuer,
         audience: audience,
         max_lifetime_seconds: max_lifetime_seconds,
         now: System.system_time(:second)
       ]}
    else
      :error
    end
  end

  defp configuration, do: Application.get_env(:commonplace_next, __MODULE__, [])
end
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Gate: every test arm a plan DECLARES must exist in the suite.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. Phase 2's plan listed "a legal verb name mounts" as a require
d
# green arm. It was never written. 31 tests passed and nothing indicated a gap:
# green-because-ABSENT is indistinguishable from green-because-PASSED, so counti
ng
# tests can never find it. Only mutating the rule did -- over-tightening the ver
b
# charset to forbid hyphens changed no test result at all.
#
# A plan that names required tests is a checklist, and a checklist nobody diffs
# against the suite is a wish. This diffs it, and FAILS.
#
# DECLARE an arm in a plan with a marker line:
#   <!-- ARM: substring that must appear in a test name -->
#
# THE MARKER IS THE CONTRACT: name the test to match the marker, not the other w
ay
# round. On its first run this gate reported 4 misses of which only ONE was real
 --
# the other three were tests that existed under different wording. A gate that
# fires on correct state is worse than no gate, because it trains people to igno
re
# it. If a marker misses, first check whether the test exists under another name
;
# if it does, the MARKER is wrong.
set -uo pipefail
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

# --self-test : prove the MATCHER cannot pass an arm that has no test.
# Offered by commonplace-dir. This gate restricts matching to `test "..."` NAMES
,
# so a token appearing in implementation code or comments must NOT satisfy an ar
m.
# That restriction was asserted and never demonstrated -- and a gate never seen
to
# fail is not known to work. This builds a corpus where every token appears ONLY
# as code and comments, plus one unrelated test, and requires every arm to MISS.
#
# BOTH ARMS OF THIS SELF-TEST ARE DEMONSTRATED:
#   matcher correct                     -> PASS, exit 0
#   matcher reads implementation text   -> "ok  widget alignment", exit 3
#
# AND A MUTATION-TESTING TRAP FOUND WHILE PROVING IT. My first attempt to break
# the matcher used a glob that did not read the file holding the tokens. The
# broken matcher therefore returned the CORRECT answer for the wrong reason, the
# self-test passed, and I briefly concluded the self-test was decoration.
# => AN INERT MUTATION IS INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM A GATE THAT WORKS.
# Mutation testing has the same vacuous-control failure as everything else:
# before believing a mutation proved something, CONFIRM THE MUTATION ACTUALLY
# PERTURBED THE THING UNDER TEST. Here that means the break must read the file
# containing the arm tokens; a break that reads nothing only proves exit 2.
# ⛔ ARGV IS A THIRD CAUSE OF "THE GATE NEVER RAN", and it was unmodelled.
# Found by commonplace-dir on their own gate: invoked wrongly it exited 1, which
# IS its missing-arms verdict -- so "I typed the command wrong" was
# indistinguishable from "the round skipped required tests." A gate returning a
# verdict about work it never examined is the exact failure it exists to catch,
# aimed at itself. This script had the same hole in a different shape: an
# unrecognised flag fell through and ran the NORMAL gate, so `--slef-test`
# reported FAIL and a caller could read that as "the self-test failed".
# Two causes were already modelled (no ARM markers, no test names). argv is a th
ird.
if [ "$#" -gt 0 ] && [ "${1:-}" != "--self-test" ]; then
  echo "FAIL: unrecognised argument '${1}'. Usage: $(basename "$0") [--self-test
]" >&2
  echo "      (exit 2 = the gate did not run. 1 would mean it ran and arms are m
issing.)" >&2
  exit 2
fi

if [ "${1:-}" = "--self-test" ]; then
  T=$(mktemp -d); trap 'rm -rf "$T"' EXIT
  mkdir -p "$T/docs" "$T/test" "$T/lib" "$T/bin"
  printf '<!-- ARM: widget alignment -->\n<!-- ARM: gasket tolerance -->\n' > "$
T/docs/p.md"
  printf '# widget alignment is handled here\ndef gasket_tolerance, do: :ok\n' >
 "$T/lib/impl.ex"
  printf '  test "something entirely unrelated" do\n  end\n' > "$T/test/a_test.e
xs"
  cp "$0" "$T/bin/"
  out=$("$T/bin/$(basename "$0")" 2>&1); rc=$?
  n_miss=$(printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -c 'MISSING')
  if [ "$rc" -eq 1 ] && [ "$n_miss" -eq 2 ]; then
    echo "SELF-TEST PASS: tokens present only in code/comments matched 0 arms (2
/2 MISSING, exit 1)."
    exit 0
  fi
  echo "SELF-TEST FAIL: expected exit 1 with 2 MISSING; got exit $rc with $n_mis
s." >&2
  printf '%s\n' "$out" >&2
  exit 3
fi

# ⭐ ARM vs ARM-PLANNED. An arm declared for a round that has NOT been dispatche
d
# is absent for a different reason than an arm missing from landed work, and
# mixing them makes RED ambiguous -- which is how a gate becomes permanently red
# and then ignored. `ARM:` = contract on landed work, and its absence FAILS.
# `ARM-PLANNED:` = contract on a future round, reported and NOT failing. Promote
# the marker to `ARM:` when that round is dispatched.
mapfile -t ARMS    < <(grep -rho '<!-- ARM: .* -->'         docs/ | sed 's/<!--
ARM: //; s/ -->//')
mapfile -t PLANNED < <(grep -rho '<!-- ARM-PLANNED: .* -->' docs/ | sed 's/<!--
ARM-PLANNED: //; s/ -->//')
if [ "${#ARMS[@]}" -eq 0 ]; then
  echo "FAIL: no ARM markers found in docs/ -- the gate has nothing to check." >
&2
  echo "      (A gate with an empty corpus passes vacuously. That is the bug thi
s file exists for.)" >&2
  exit 2
fi

TESTS=$(grep -rho '^\s*test "[^"]*"' test/ | sed 's/.*test "//; s/"$//')
N_TESTS=$(printf '%s\n' "$TESTS" | grep -c .)
if [ "$N_TESTS" -eq 0 ]; then
  echo "FAIL: no test names found under test/ -- wrong referent, not an empty su
ite." >&2
  exit 2
fi

# ── MODULE COVERAGE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⛔ WHY. Two repos found unbriefed Sol machinery within an hour: a DocHost.Redu
cer
# here, an async checkpoint worker in commonplace-dir against a brief forbidding
# "timers, debounce, scheduler, watches" -- none of which it was.
# ⭐ AN ENUMERATION IS SATISFIABLE BY ANYTHING NOT ON THE LIST. My phase-3 plan
# listed what was in scope and what was out; DocHost.Reducer was on neither list
# and was therefore permitted by omission. "Out of scope: A, B, C" is not a
# property, it is a denylist, and a denylist of machinery is the same defect as
a
# denylist of secrets.
# => The property: EVERY MODULE IN lib/ IS DECLARED IN A PLAN. Adding one is fin
e
#    -- adding one SILENTLY is not. Declare with:  <!-- MODULE: Full.Module.Name
 -->
mapfile -t DECLARED < <(grep -rho '<!-- MODULE: .* -->' docs/ | sed 's/<!-- MODU
LE: //; s/ -->//')
# ⚠️  SCOPE, DECLARED (markdown/biscuit/cell, 2026-08-27): the `^` anchors this t
o COLUMN-0
#    defmodules. A module NESTED inside another (indented) is invisible to it. M
easured
#    2026-08-27 22:06Z: `grep -rn '^\s\+defmodule ' lib/` -> 0, so the scope cos
ts nothing
#    TODAY and is a gap the day someone nests one. ⭐ A line-anchored selector c
annot see a
#    construct that does not start the line -- and widening the ALTERNATION does
 not fix a
#    defect in the SHAPE (their `cond do` case: the added keyword could never ma
tch).
#    ⛔ Do not relax the anchor without a red arm: unanchored, this matches `def
module` inside
#    strings, comments and heredocs, trading a false zero for a false alarm.
mapfile -t ACTUAL   < <(grep -rho '^defmodule [A-Za-z0-9_.]*' lib/ | sed 's/defm
odule //' | sort -u)
undeclared=0
if [ "${#ACTUAL[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
  for m in "${ACTUAL[@]}"; do
    hit=0
    for d in "${DECLARED[@]:-}"; do [ "$d" = "$m" ] && hit=1 && break; done
    [ "$hit" -eq 1 ] || { printf '  UNDECLARED MODULE  %s\n' "$m"; undeclared=$(
(undeclared+1)); }
  done
fi

miss=0
for arm in "${ARMS[@]}"; do
  # ⛔ HERESTRING, NOT `printf | grep -q`. Under pipefail the old form was a
  # false-MISS race: printf writes one line per write(), grep -q exits on the
  # first match, the next write() gets EPIPE/SIGPIPE, the pipeline fails, and a
  # PRESENT arm reports MISSING. Found by commonplace-dir (18:28Z) in the siblin
g
  # of this script; MEASURED here on an unchanged tree: 12/150 false FAIL with
  # the pipe, 0/150 with the herestring. It had shown up earlier today as a
  # "transient 1 missing" and was dismissed as mid-priming noise. A gate that
  # sometimes goes red on correct state is worse than no gate: it trains people
  # to re-run it until green.
  if grep -qiF -- "$arm" <<< "$TESTS"; then
    printf '  ok      %s\n' "$arm"
  else
    printf '  MISSING %s\n' "$arm"; miss=$((miss+1))
  fi
done

echo "---"
# ⛔ A MARKER DECLARES INTENT; THE TAG DECIDES WHETHER THE ARM RUNS. Ported from
 markdown's
# ce8dde9 (its FIRST version was blind to @moduletag, which is the defect the pa
tch exists to fix
# surviving inside the patch). @moduletag excludes EVERY test in the module -- o
ne line can switch
# off a whole file of declared arms while this gate still prints "ok" for each o
f them.
# ⚠️  KNOWN GAP, MEASURED HERE 2026-08-27 AND NOT CLOSED BY THIS BLOCK: it reads
exclusions from
# test/test_helper.exs ONLY, so a CLI `mix test --exclude <tag>` is invisible to
 it. Measured:
# `mix test --exclude integration` skipped 60 tests and this gate still printed
missing: 0, PASS.
# ⏳ RETIREMENT: delete this block when the gate derives its population from an
ExUnit RUN's own
# output instead of grepping source -- that closes both this and the CLI gap at
once.
EXCLUDED_TAGS=$(sed -n 's/^[[:space:]]*ExUnit.configure(exclude:[[:space:]]*\[\(
.*\)\]).*/\1/p' test/test_helper.exs 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' :' | tr ',' ' ')
TAGGED=$(awk '
  # ⛔ COLUMN-0 ONLY. A NESTED defmodule (a test double declared inside the test
 module) must NOT end
  # the module-tag scope: doc-sync measured that resetting on any defmodule made
 a real @moduletag
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Run a command, keep ALL of its output in a file, print the summary line and th
e FILE'S PATH,
# and exit with THE COMMAND'S rc.
#
# Usage: bin/capture-run.sh [--overwrite] [--time|--no-time] <label> <cmd> [args
...]
#
# ⛔⛔ WHY THIS IS A SHARED SCRIPT AND NOT A RULE. Three runners in this reposit
ory have piped a
# suite through `tail`, and `[measured 2026-09-03]` that truncation cost the IDE
NTITY of a failure
# three times in one day -- once in `bump-pin.sh`, twice in ad-hoc runners writt
en the same hour by
# someone who had just filed the finding. ExUnit prints its FAILURE BLOCKS BEFOR
E the summary, so a
# tail keeps "N tests, K failures" and drops WHICH.
# ⭐ The correction already existed at `land-round.sh:38-42`, written 2026-08-25
 for this exact
# reason. It could not help the others because AN ARTIFACT ONLY FIRES WHERE IT I
S INSTALLED: a fix
# filed inside one runner does not reach the next runner anybody writes. One scr
ipt, called by all
# of them, is the only version of this that cannot drift per caller.
#
# ⛔ AND THE rc IS THE COMMAND'S. `cmd | tee log` exits with tee's status, and `
cmd | tail -1`
# exits with tail's -- both 0 whichever way the suite went. This runs the comman
d with its output
# REDIRECTED, never piped, so `$?` is the thing you asked about.
#
# ⛔⛔ AND THE LABEL IS A FILENAME, SO TWO RUNS UNDER ONE LABEL ARE ONE FILE. `[
measured 2026-09-04]`
# three runs sharing the label `org2b-fullsuite` left one log: the 539-tests/12-
failures run that
# was the whole reason to look was overwritten by the run that came after it, an
d the overwrite was
# SILENT. ⭐ That is this script's own subject aimed at itself -- it exists beca
use truncation
# destroys the identity of a failure, and it was destroying whole failures by a
different mechanism.
# ⇒ An existing log is REFUSED with rc 73 (EX_CANTCREAT) BEFORE the command runs
, so a refusal
# never costs the run it was protecting and never half-writes the file it was pr
otecting.
# ⭐ `--overwrite` is the explicit escape, because a caller with STABLE labels b
y design is not
# making this mistake; it must say so in argv rather than have the guard guess.
`bin/bump-pin.sh` is
# the one such caller in the tree (`[measured]` 1 of 1 in `bin/`, 0 in `test/`).
set -u

overwrite=0
timing=1
while :; do
  case "${1:-}" in
    --overwrite) overwrite=1; shift ;;
    --time)      timing=1;    shift ;;
    --no-time)   timing=0;    shift ;;
    *) break ;;
  esac
done

label="${1:?usage: capture-run.sh [--overwrite] [--time|--no-time] <label> <cmd>
 [args...]}"; shift
[ "$#" -gt 0 ] || { echo "capture-run.sh: no command given" >&2; exit 64; }

dir="${CAPTURE_RUN_DIR:-tmp/run-logs}"
mkdir -p "$dir" || { echo "capture-run.sh: cannot create $dir" >&2; exit 64; }
log="$dir/$(printf '%s' "$label" | tr -c 'A-Za-z0-9' '_').log"
load="${log%.log}.load"

# ⛔ THE GUARD RUNS BEFORE THE COMMAND, and that ordering is the point: refusing
 after the run would
# have already destroyed the file, and refusing after a partial write would leav
e a log that is
# neither run's. Nothing is executed and nothing is written on this path.
if [ -e "$log" ] && [ "$overwrite" -eq 0 ]; then
  echo "capture-run.sh: REFUSED -- a log already exists for label '$label':" >&2
  echo "    $log" >&2
  echo "  It was NOT overwritten and the command was NOT run." >&2
  echo "  Use a different label, or pass --overwrite as the first argument if th
e reuse is deliberate." >&2
  exit 73
fi

if [ -e "$load" ] && [ "$overwrite" -eq 0 ]; then
  echo "capture-run.sh: REFUSED -- a load file already exists for label '$label'
:" >&2
  echo "    $load" >&2
  echo "  It was NOT overwritten and the command was NOT run." >&2
  exit 73
fi

# ⛔⛔ TIMING GOES TO ITS OWN FILE, NEVER INTO THE SUITE LOG. `bin/failure-ordin
al.sh` recovers a
# failure's ordinal by counting CLIFormatter progress dots and REFUSES unless
# `dots == tests - failures - excluded`; interleaving timing lines is precisely
the "written by a
# formatter this reader does not understand" case it exits 2 on.
# ⭐ Turning one instrument on must not cost the repository another one it alrea
dy had. The arm for
# that is `failure-ordinal.sh` calibrating on a --time log exactly as it does wi
thout it.
times="${log%.log}.times"
if [ "$timing" -eq 1 ]; then
  if [ -e "$times" ] && [ "$overwrite" -eq 0 ]; then
    echo "capture-run.sh: REFUSED -- a timing file already exists for label '$la
bel':" >&2
    echo "    $times" >&2
    echo "  It was NOT overwritten and the command was NOT run." >&2
    exit 73
  fi
  export CAPTURE_RUN_TIMES="$times"
  : > "$times"
else
  # ⛔⛔ EXPLICITLY OFF MEANS OFF, AND AN INHERITED ENV VAR IS NOT OFF. `[measur
ed 2026-09-04]` the
  # full suite ran under `--time`, so every child this suite spawned INHERITED C
APTURE_RUN_TIMES and
  # a `capture-run.sh` invoked WITHOUT `--time` still had it set. ⇒ A nested cap
tured run would have
  # written into its ANCESTOR's timing file: two runs, one file -- this script's
 own subject, one
  # process boundary out, and the guard above cannot see it because the path was
 never argv's.
  # ⭐ Found only because the suite itself was run under --time; under a plain r
un the arm asserting
  # this passes VACUOUSLY.
  unset CAPTURE_RUN_TIMES
fi

# A load witness answers a different question from the pre-run load gate: what e
lse was on the box
# while this captured run happened? It is evidence only. None of these commands
can refuse the run.
# `ps` includes standing tenants that box-free deliberately excludes; its %CPU i
s labelled as the
# process lifetime average. `top`'s first frame has that same meaning, so only i
ts second frame is
# retained as the instantaneous three-second sample.
boxfree="${CAPTURE_RUN_BOXFREE:-/home/jes/boss-clod/box-free.sh}"
capture_load_block() {
  phase="$1"
  printf '%s %s\n' "$phase" "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)" >> "$load"

  echo '[box-free]' >> "$load"
  if [ ! -x "$boxfree" ]; then
    echo 'ABSENT' >> "$load"
  else
    bash "$boxfree" >> "$load" 2>&1
    boxfree_rc=$?
    [ "$boxfree_rc" -eq 2 ] && echo 'BLIND rc=2' >> "$load"
    printf 'box-free rc=%s\n' "$boxfree_rc" >> "$load"
  fi

  echo '[lifetime-avg] ps -eo pcpu,pid,comm --sort=-pcpu | head -12' >> "$load"
  ps -eo pcpu,pid,comm --sort=-pcpu 2>&1 | head -12 >> "$load" || echo 'ABSENT'
>> "$load"

  echo '[instant-3s] second frame of top -b -n 2 -d 3 -o %CPU, process rows, hea
d -12' >> "$load"
  if command -v top >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    top -b -n 2 -d 3 -o %CPU 2>&1 | awk '
      /^top -/ { frame++; rows=0; in_rows=0; next }
      frame == 2 && /^[[:space:]]*PID[[:space:]]/ { in_rows=1; next }
      frame == 2 && in_rows && rows < 12 && NF { print; rows++ }
    ' >> "$load"
  else
    echo 'ABSENT' >> "$load"
  fi

  if [ -r /proc/loadavg ]; then
    printf 'load1=' >> "$load"
    tr -d '\n' < /proc/loadavg >> "$load"
    echo ' (1-min decaying avg, lags bursts)' >> "$load"
  else
    echo 'load1=ABSENT (1-min decaying avg, lags bursts)' >> "$load"
  fi
}

: > "$load"
capture_load_block START

rc=0
"$@" > "$log" 2>&1 || rc=$?
capture_load_block END

summary=$(grep -oE '[0-9]+ (tests?|doctests?), [0-9]+ failures?[^|]*' "$log" | t
ail -n 1)
[ -n "$summary" ] || summary=$(tail -n 1 "$log")

if [ "$rc" -eq 0 ]; then
  printf 'ok    %-14s %s   [full log: %s]\n' "$label" "$summary" "$log"
  if [ "$timing" -eq 1 ] && [ -f "$times" ]; then
    time_lines=$(wc -l < "$times")
    [ "$time_lines" -gt 0 ] && time_rows=$((time_lines - 1)) || time_rows=0
    printf '      per-test times (%s rows): %s\n' "$time_rows" "$times"
  fi
else
  printf 'RED   %-14s rc=%s %s   [FULL LOG: %s]\n' "$label" "$rc" "$summary" "$l
og"
  # ⭐ Names are RECOVERED, not hoped for in a tail. A failing run that names no
 test is itself
  # information and says so, rather than printing nothing and looking like a qui
et failure.
  echo "failing tests (full output in $log):"
  grep -E '^\s+[0-9]+\) (test|doctest)' "$log" || echo "  (no test-name lines --
 see the tail below)"
  # ⛔⛔ AND THE TAIL IS PRINTED WHATEVER THE SHAPE, because the name-grep is a
claim about what the
  # failure LOOKS like. A compile error, a `setup_all` crash or an ExUnit-level
abort matches no
  # `N) test` line at all -- so a runner anchored only on that pattern is least
helpful exactly when
  # the breakage is worst, which is the wrong gradient. (Bound named by hermes,
2026-09-03, from the
  # same defect in its own runner.) Nothing is ever lost either way: the whole o
utput is on disk.
  echo "--- last 40 lines (full output in $log) ---"
  tail -n 40 "$log"
fi

exit "$rc"
